  "usecase": "A UseCase encapsulates a single business logic operation. It makes code modular and testable. Often used in clean architecture.\n\nInterview-ready explanation: Start by defining what the concept is and the problem it solves. For “What is the `UseCase` (interactor) pattern?”, explain where it belongs in an Android application's architecture or lifecycle, then describe the normal implementation and the important constraints. Mention lifecycle ownership, threading, state restoration, security, or resource usage when relevant rather than treating the API in isolation.\n\nPractical considerations: Prefer current AndroidX/Jetpack APIs and Kotlin patterns for new development. Check API-level and library-version differences before relying on behavior that changed over time. Keep UI work on the main thread, move blocking I/O or CPU-heavy work off it, handle cancellation and configuration changes deliberately, and test failure paths as well as the happy path.\n\nInterview tip: A strong answer should connect the definition to a concrete example, explain one trade-off or limitation, and state which modern alternative you would choose when the older API is deprecated or unsuitable.",
  "clean-architecture": "Clean Architecture separates the app into layers: Data, Domain, and Presentation. It enforces dependency inversion (domain independent of frameworks). It improves maintainability and testability.\n\nInterview-ready explanation: Start by defining what the concept is and the problem it solves. For “What is Clean Architecture?”, explain where it belongs in an Android application's architecture or lifecycle, then describe the normal implementation and the important constraints. Mention lifecycle ownership, threading, state restoration, security, or resource usage when relevant rather than treating the API in isolation.\n\nPractical considerations: Prefer current AndroidX/Jetpack APIs and Kotlin patterns for new development. Check API-level and library-version differences before relying on behavior that changed over time. Keep UI work on the main thread, move blocking I/O or CPU-heavy work off it, handle cancellation and configuration changes deliberately, and test failure paths as well as the happy path.\n\nInterview tip: A strong answer should connect the definition to a concrete example, explain one trade-off or limitation, and state which modern alternative you would choose when the older API is deprecated or unsuitable.",
