  "permissions": "android.permission.INTERNET allows the app to open network sockets and is a normal permission granted at install time. android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE lets the app query information about network connectivity and state; it is also a normal permission. ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE is not required merely to make an HTTP request. Therefore, an app making network calls normally needs INTERNET, while it needs ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE only when it also needs to inspect network state.\n\nInterview-ready explanation: Start by defining what the concept is and the problem it solves. For “What is the difference between `android.permission.INTERNET` and `android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE`?”, explain where it belongs in an Android application's architecture or lifecycle, then describe the normal implementation and the important constraints. Mention lifecycle ownership, threading, state restoration, security, or resource usage when relevant rather than treating the API in isolation.\n\nPractical considerations: Prefer current AndroidX/Jetpack APIs and Kotlin patterns for new development. Check API-level and library-version differences before relying on behavior that changed over time. Keep UI work on the main thread, move blocking I/O or CPU-heavy work off it, handle cancellation and configuration changes deliberately, and test failure paths as well as the happy path.\n\nInterview tip: A strong answer should connect the definition to a concrete example, explain one trade-off or limitation, and state which modern alternative you would choose when the older API is deprecated or unsuitable.",
  "dangerous-permissions": "Android classifies permissions by protection level. Dangerous permissions protect sensitive data or actions, such as camera, location, microphone, and certain storage/media access, and many must be requested at runtime on Android 6.0/API 23 and later when the app targets the relevant API levels. The exact permission set and runtime behavior can change across Android releases, so applications should request only the permissions they actually need and explain the user-facing purpose before requesting them.\n\nInterview-ready explanation: Start by defining what the concept is and the problem it solves. For “What is the `Dangerous` permission level?”, explain where it belongs in an Android application's architecture or lifecycle, then describe the normal implementation and the important constraints. Mention lifecycle ownership, threading, state restoration, security, or resource usage when relevant rather than treating the API in isolation.\n\nPractical considerations: Prefer current AndroidX/Jetpack APIs and Kotlin patterns for new development. Check API-level and library-version differences before relying on behavior that changed over time. Keep UI work on the main thread, move blocking I/O or CPU-heavy work off it, handle cancellation and configuration changes deliberately, and test failure paths as well as the happy path.\n\nInterview tip: A strong answer should connect the definition to a concrete example, explain one trade-off or limitation, and state which modern alternative you would choose when the older API is deprecated or unsuitable.",
