  "secure-password-storage": "An Android app should not store a user's plaintext password, and simply encrypting a password in local storage does not make password storage a good authentication design. In a server-backed system, send credentials over HTTPS to the authentication service and let the server store passwords using a strong password-hashing scheme. On the device, store only the minimum authentication material needed, such as a short-lived token or refresh credential, protected appropriately. Android Keystore is useful for protecting cryptographic keys; it is not a password database.\n\nInterview-ready explanation: Start by defining what the concept is and the problem it solves. For “How do you store passwords securely?”, explain where it belongs in an Android application's architecture or lifecycle, then describe the normal implementation and the important constraints. Mention lifecycle ownership, threading, state restoration, security, or resource usage when relevant rather than treating the API in isolation.\n\nPractical considerations: Prefer current AndroidX/Jetpack APIs and Kotlin patterns for new development. Check API-level and library-version differences before relying on behavior that changed over time. Keep UI work on the main thread, move blocking I/O or CPU-heavy work off it, handle cancellation and configuration changes deliberately, and test failure paths as well as the happy path.\n\nInterview tip: A strong answer should connect the definition to a concrete example, explain one trade-off or limitation, and state which modern alternative you would choose when the older API is deprecated or unsuitable.",
