  "workmanager-vs-jobscheduler": "WorkManager provides a unified API for deferrable background work, compatible with all API levels. It chooses the best underlying scheduler (JobScheduler, AlarmManager, etc.).\n\nInterview-ready explanation: Start by defining what the concept is and the problem it solves. For “What is WorkManager and how does it differ from JobScheduler?”, explain where it belongs in an Android application's architecture or lifecycle, then describe the normal implementation and the important constraints. Mention lifecycle ownership, threading, state restoration, security, or resource usage when relevant rather than treating the API in isolation.\n\nPractical considerations: Prefer current AndroidX/Jetpack APIs and Kotlin patterns for new development. Check API-level and library-version differences before relying on behavior that changed over time. Keep UI work on the main thread, move blocking I/O or CPU-heavy work off it, handle cancellation and configuration changes deliberately, and test failure paths as well as the happy path.\n\nInterview tip: A strong answer should connect the definition to a concrete example, explain one trade-off or limitation, and state which modern alternative you would choose when the older API is deprecated or unsuitable.",
  "livedata-advantages": "LiveData is a lifecycle-aware observable data holder. Observers associated with a LifecycleOwner receive updates only while their lifecycle is in an active state, which reduces common UI lifecycle mistakes. LiveData does not automatically eliminate every null or memory-leak problem, and it does not replace proper lifecycle management. It is particularly useful in existing View-based Android applications, while modern Kotlin-first applications often expose StateFlow or Flow from ViewModels.\n\nInterview-ready explanation: Start by defining what the concept is and the problem it solves. For “What is LiveData and its advantages?”, explain where it belongs in an Android application's architecture or lifecycle, then describe the normal implementation and the important constraints. Mention lifecycle ownership, threading, state restoration, security, or resource usage when relevant rather than treating the API in isolation.\n\nPractical considerations: Prefer current AndroidX/Jetpack APIs and Kotlin patterns for new development. Check API-level and library-version differences before relying on behavior that changed over time. Keep UI work on the main thread, move blocking I/O or CPU-heavy work off it, handle cancellation and configuration changes deliberately, and test failure paths as well as the happy path.\n\nInterview tip: A strong answer should connect the definition to a concrete example, explain one trade-off or limitation, and state which modern alternative you would choose when the older API is deprecated or unsuitable.",
